                "text": "The rule: given input grid, output same size. For each red (1) cell, put yellow (4) at diagonal neighbors. Wait, natural language says \"Where there's a red square, put yellow squares at it's corners (4 total).\" But verifier: x0 = ofcolor(I, ONE) red? Actually 1=blue, 2=red in ARC. Let me check colors: 0 black, 1 blue, 2 red, 3 green, 4 yellow, 6 magenta, 7 orange.\n\nThe verifier: x0 = color 1 (blue), x1 = color 2 (red). x2 = dneighbors of x0 \u2192 orange (7) at direct neighbors of blue. x3 = ineighbors of x1 \u2192 yellow (4) at diagonal neighbors of red.\n\nThe natural language is somewhat inconsistent with verifier but verifier is ground truth.\n\nSo: for each blue (1), place orange (7) at 4 orthogonal neighbors. For each red (2), place yellow (4) at 4 diagonal neighbors. Preserve original cells.\n\nWait natural language: \"Where there's a red square, put yellow squares at it's corners\" \u2014 yes matches 2\u2192diagonal\u21924. \"Where there's a dark blue square, put orange squares directly above and below it (4 total)\" \u2014 hmm says above/below but 4 total, verifier dneighbors = 4 orthogonal. Keep.\n\nAlso note original color cells are preserved (fill only adds). Also fill doesn't overwrite? fill sets value at those indices; if a neighbor is itself a colored cell, it would be overwritten. Order: fill 7 then fill 4, so 4 overwrites 7 potentially.\n\nI need to create 3 demonstration pairs. Inputs must not place cells too close to edges (neighbors out of bounds are clipped by fill, but ofcolor includes all). Actually neighbors outside grid are just ignored by fill bounds check. Fine, but better to place interior.\n\nLet me create inputs with blue and red separated so no interference.\n\nPair 1: single blue at center of 7x7.\nInput 7x7 all 0, blue at (3,3).\nOutput: orange at (2,3),(4,3),(3,2),(3,4).\n\nPair 2: single red at center of 7x7 \u2192 yellow at diagonals.\n\nPair 3: mix, e.g., 9x9 with blue at (2,2), red at (6,6), maybe magenta and light blue (6 magenta? \"light blue or magenta\" - color 6 is magenta). Include 6 to show it's untouched.\n\nLet me include a magenta cell and maybe ignore. Colors: 6 magenta, 8 light blue (azure). Include 8 too.\n\nLet me build pair 3: 9x9 all zero, blue at (2,2), red at (6,6), magenta at (4,7), azure at (7,1). Wait azure=8, magenta=6. Do nothing with them.\n\nCheck overlaps: blue (2,2) orthogonal neighbors (1,2),(3,2),(2,1),(2,3). Red (6,6) diagonals (5,5),(5,7),(7,5),(7,7). (7,7) fine. Magenta (4,7) untouched. Azure (7,1) untouched. No conflicts.\n\nPair 1: blue at (3,3) in 7x7. Orthogonal neighbors: (2,3),(4,3),(3,2),(3,4).\nPair 2: red at (3,3). Diagonals (2,2),(2,4),(4,2),(4,4).\n\nLet me call show_transformed_input three times.\n\nActually I can do multiple calls in one block. Let me construct grids.\n\nPair1 input 7x7:\nrow0: 0000000\nrow1: 0000000\nrow2: 0000000\nrow3: 0001000  -> blue at col3\nrow4: 0000000\nrow5: 0000000\nrow6: 0000000\nWait blue=1. col index 3. row \"[0,0,0,1,0,0,0]\".\n\nPair2 input 7x7 red=2 at (3,3): row3 [0,0,0,2,0,0,0].\n\nPair3 9x9: row2 blue at (2,2): [0,0,1,0,0,0,0,0,0]. row6 red at (6,6): [0,0,0,0,0,0,2,0,0]. row4 magenta at (4,7): [0,0,0,0,0,0,0,6,0]. row7 azure at (7,1): [0,8,0,0,0,0,0,0,0].\n\nLet me submit.",
